`unique` preserves first-occurrence order and uses a hash set internally, so this is O(n). Collecting to `Char`s first keeps multibyte characters intact. It is case-sensitive; lowercase the input first if you want `a` and `A` treated as the same.

```julia
function with_commas(n::Integer)
    s = string(abs(widen(n)))
    groups = [s[max(1, i - 2):i] for i in reverse(length(s):-3:1)]
    return (n < 0 ? "-" : "") * join(groups, ',')
end

# Checks
@assert with_commas(1234567) == "1,234,567"
@assert with_commas(-1234) == "-1,234"
@assert with_commas(big(10)^20) == "100,000,000,000,000,000,000"
@assert with_commas(typemin(Int8)) == "-128"
```

Slicing the digit string into groups of three from the right (`length(s):-3:1` gives the end of each group) and joining with commas is O(digits). `widen` before `abs` matters because `abs(typemin(Int8))` overflows back to `-128`; widening first keeps the magnitude correct for every fixed-width type, and is a no-op for `BigInt`.

